WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.033 --> 00:00:04.653
A mouse reels as it walks.

2
00:00:05.533 --> 00:00:07.873
Is that a walking problem, or a building problem?

3
00:00:08.467 --> 00:00:12.587
In 1951, Douglas Falconer named this mutant reeler.

4
00:00:13.207 --> 00:00:14.907
Its cortex was the deeper puzzle.

5
00:00:15.550 --> 00:00:20.270
First, direction. Deep means near the ventricle, where neurons are born.

6
00:00:21.230 --> 00:00:23.190
Superficial means near the outer surface.

7
00:00:23.833 --> 00:00:27.593
The first neurons form a thin sheet under the surface, the preplate.

8
00:00:28.433 --> 00:00:29.973
The next neurons split it in two.

9
00:00:30.567 --> 00:00:34.027
Above sits the marginal zone, with the Cajal-Retzius cells.

10
00:00:35.167 --> 00:00:36.167
Below sits the subplate.

11
00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:41.650
Each later wave climbs past the waves before it, and settles just under the marginal

12
00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:42.650
zone.

13
00:00:42.683 --> 00:00:44.993
So the cortex is built inside-out.

14
00:00:44.993 --> 00:00:49.283
Early-born neurons end up deep, and late-born neurons near the surface.

15
00:00:49.933 --> 00:00:54.033
Is reeler making the wrong neurons, or putting the right ones in the wrong place?

16
00:00:54.667 --> 00:00:57.707
Verne Caviness and Richard Sidman used birthdating.

17
00:00:58.607 --> 00:01:02.947
A radioactive building block of DNA labels cells copying their DNA.

18
00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:06.760
Each division shares the label between two daughter cells.

19
00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:11.420
A cell that divides once more, then never again, stays heavily labelled.

20
00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:16.720
Predict. If reeler made the wrong neurons, what would birthdating show?

21
00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:19.120
And if it misplaced the right ones?

22
00:01:24.683 --> 00:01:29.463
In 1973: the same classes of neurons, born on the same schedule.

23
00:01:30.067 --> 00:01:31.687
What changed was their order.

24
00:01:32.627 --> 00:01:36.667
Early-born neurons tended to sit near the surface, and late-born neurons deep.

25
00:01:37.217 --> 00:01:40.197
The pattern was roughly inverted, with a lot of scatter.

26
00:01:42.867 --> 00:01:46.327
Why? In reeler, the preplate fails to split.

27
00:01:47.487 --> 00:01:50.327
Later waves tend to stop beneath the neurons already there.

28
00:01:50.867 --> 00:01:53.867
So reeler is a defect of placement, not production.

29
00:01:54.787 --> 00:01:58.887
The missing protein, Reelin, is needed to stack that birth order the right way up.

30
00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:03.620
With Reelin signalling, each new wave tends to finish its climb at the top.

31
00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:07.000
Without it, newcomers tend to stop beneath their elders.

32
00:02:07.750 --> 00:02:10.990
Takeaway: made on schedule, stacked the wrong way up.

33
00:02:10.990 --> 00:02:13.270
Next, where does Reelin come from?
